% The anti-double-dipping rule from 00-eligibility-paradox.dl, done in
% a way that terminates — and the fix is a policy decision, not a
% syntactic trick.
%
%   python3 datalog.py --trace programs/eligibility-stable.dl
%
% The broken version asked "is anyone in this household ALREADY
% claiming?" where 'claiming' was this program's own output.  The fix is
% to read a REGISTER instead: claims recorded by whatever process
% actually grants them.  That is an input to this program and never its
% output, so the loop cannot close, the program stratifies, and the
% answer is determinate.
%
% Worth noticing in --trace: everything lands in ONE stratum.  Both
% negations here point at base facts (claim_on_record, employed), and
% base facts are complete before evaluation begins — so negating them
% costs nothing and forces no ordering.  Only negating a *derived*
% predicate creates a stratum boundary, because only then does the
% engine have to finish computing something before it can ask what is
% missing from it.  That is the whole difference between this program
% and the paradox: not how much negation, but what the negation reads.

member(bob,   oak_house).
member(cyril, oak_house).
member(dana,  elm_house).
member(edith, elm_house).

receives_pension(cyril).
receives_pension(edith).
employed(dana).

% The register: households whose claim is already on file elsewhere.
claim_on_record(elm_house).

qualifying_household(H) :- member(P, H), receives_pension(P),
                           not claim_on_record(H).

eligible(P) :- member(P, H), qualifying_household(H), not employed(P).
